e). So maybe not. Alternatively, B2, B3, B4 (but I don't have B3 or B4). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe focus on pungs. I have two B2s. If I can get another, that's a pung. But I have to keep one B2. If I discard a B2 now, maybe someone else can peng. But the next player has a peng of B1, so they might not need B2. The player across has a kong of W8. Not sure. The previous player has no melds with B2.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. For example, F4. I have one F4. Since there are three F4s left, maybe it's safe to discard. But F4 is the North Wind, which is my seat wind. If I discard F4, and someone has a pung of North Wind, they could get points. But since I don't have any other F4s, maybe it's okay. However, since I have one, maybe keeping it could be useful if I can form a pung later. But there's only one in my hand.\n\nAnother thought: the game history shows that I have played some tiles before, like F1, F2, J2, etc. Maybe some tiles are already out. For example, F1 has been played by me and others. The next player has played J1, and so on.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, F4 has 3 left. So discarding F4 now would leave 2 remaining. If someone needs it, they might take it, but since I only have one, maybe it's not critical.\n\nAlternatively, playing T3 might be a good idea. The T1, T2, T3 is a chow. If I discard T3, maybe someone can chi. The previous player has a chow of T3, T4, T5. If I discard T3, they might already have it, so they can't chi again. Unless they have T2 and T4 to make a chow. But the previous player's meld is T3, T4, T5, so they already have T3. So maybe discarding T3 is safe. But if I discard T3, I lose the chow. Wait, I have T1, T2, T3 in my hand. If I discard T3, I can't form that chow anymore unless I draw another T3. But since I have T1 and T2, maybe keeping T3 is better to form the chow.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard a W7. I have two W7s. If I discard one, maybe someone can peng. But the next