removes a non-terminal tile, which could help in aiming for All Terminals and Honors if the player can eliminate the other non-terminals. However, the hand still has W4, W5, B2. So maybe not. Alternatively, discarding B2 would remove another non-terminal. But the player has B5 and B2. If they discard B5, they can still keep B2 for possible chows. So maybe keep B2 and discard B5.\n\nAnother angle: the player has F4 (West) as a single. Since the Prevailing Wind is West, having a pung of F4 would give 2 points. The player should keep F4 to try to get another one. So discard B5 instead of F4.\n\nSo the best action is to discard B5. That's one of the valid actions.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The player should discard B5 to remove a non-terminal tile and focus on potential patterns like All Terminals and Honors or concealed pungs. B5 is a single and not part of any chow or pung, making it a safe discard.  \nReason: Discarding B5 eliminates a non-terminal tile, aiding in forming All Terminals and Honors (32 points) or other high-value patterns. Keeping W7 pair and F4 (Prevailing Wind) for potential pung points is strategic.  \nAnswer: Play B5"}
