-7. Since T4 and T5 are in the previous player's melds (T3, T4, T5), maybe T6 is less needed. But I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, T3 is part of the T1-2-3 chow. If I discard T3, that would break that chow. So better not to discard T3.\n\nSimilarly, T2 is part of the chow. Discarding T2 would break it. So better keep T2.\n\nW4 and W5 are part of a possible chow with W6. If I discard W4 or W5, that might break that possibility. So maybe keep them.\n\nW7 is a pair. Discarding one W7 would leave only one, which is not useful. So better not discard W7.\n\nT1 is part of the chow. Discarding T1 would break the chow. So better keep.\n\nF4 and J3 are single honors. If I discard one of them, maybe it's safe. J3 is a White Dragon. The previous player has a J3 pung. If I discard J3, they might take it for a kong. But since I have only one J3, maybe it's safe. However, if I discard J3, they could add to their pung, but since they already have a pung, adding a fourth would be a kong. But the unshown tiles have J3:1, so there's only one left. If I discard J3, they can take it. So maybe that's a risk.\n\nAlternatively, F4 is a North Wind. My seat wind is North, so having a pung of F4 (North Wind) would give me Seat Wind points. But I only have one F4. If I discard it, I lose the chance to form a pung. But since there are F4:3 left in unshown tiles, maybe it's possible to get another. However, if I discard F4 now, others might take it. Since the next player has F1, F2, F3 pungs, but F4 is not in their melds. The player across has F4 in their history (Play F4). Not sure.\n\nSo, considering all this, the best tiles to discard are those that are not part of any potential meld. B2, F4, J3 are single honor tiles. Between them, B2 is a Dot 2, which is a middle tile. If I discard B2, it's less likely to be needed for a chow. Alternatively, J3 is a White Dragon, and if discarded, the next player (who has a J3 pung) could take it for a kong. But since there's only one J3 left, if I discard it, they can take it. However, if I keep it, I might not be able to form a pung. But since I only have one, maybe it's better to discard it to avoid giving them a kong.\n\nAlternatively, F4 is a North Wind. Since my seat wind is North, having a pung of F4 would give me 2 points (Seat Wind). But I only have one. So if I discard F4, I lose that chance. But if I keep it, maybe I can get another F4. However, there are 3 F4 left, so it's possible. But if I discard F4, others might take it. The next player has F1, F2, F3 pungs, so they might not need F4. The player across has F4 in their history, but not sure.\n\nSo between B2, F4, J3, which is the best to discard. B2 is the least useful, as it's a single Dot. F4 could be useful for my seat wind. J3 is a White Dragon, and if discarded, the next player could take it for a kong. So maybe discard B2. Alternatively, discard J3 if I think it's safe.\n\nAnother option is to discard T6. T6 is a single tile. If I discard T6, it might be part of someone's chow. For example, T4-5-6 or T5-6-7. Since the previous player has T3, T4, T5, they might need T6 for T4-5-6. But they already have that chow. Maybe not. So T6 might be less needed.\n\nSo possible options are Play B2, Play F4, Play J3, Play T6.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles:\n\n- B2 has 2 left. If I discard B2, there are still 2 left. So it's safe.\n\n- F4 has 3 left. If I discard F4, others might take it. But since my seat wind is North, keeping F4 could help. But I only have one. If I discard it, I can't form a pung. So maybe better to keep it.\n\n- J3 has 1 left. If I discard it, the next player can take it for a kong. But since they already have a J3 pung, adding a fourth would be a kong, which gives them points. So I should avoid giving them that.\n\n- T6 has 2 left. Discarding it might be safe.\n\nSo between B2 and T6. Which one is better to discard. If I discard B2, it's a single tile that's not part of any meld. If I discard T6, it's a middle tile that could be part of a chow. But since I don't have T4 or T5 in my hand (wait, I have T2, T3, but not T4 or T5?), no, I have T3 in my hand. Wait, the previous player has a chow of T3, T4, T5. So they already have that. So T6 might not be needed for their chow. So maybe T6 is safe to discard.\n\nBut I have T6 in my hand. If I discard T6, maybe someone else can use it. For example, if they have T5 and T7, they can form a chow. But I don't know. It's safer to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. So B2 is a better candidate.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to discard B2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential for multiple chows and pairs. Discarding B2, a single tile not part of any meld, minimizes risk and maintains flexibility for forming concealed melds.]\nReason: [B2 is a single tile with no immediate use in the current hand's structure. Discarding it avoids giving others useful tiles while preserving key tiles for potential chows (e.g., T1-2-3, W4-5-6) and pairs (W7, T1).]\nAnswer: Play B2"}
