other options, playing B8 or T6. Let's see the unshown counts: B8 has 2 remaining. T6 has 2 remaining. So both are similar. Maybe playing B8 is better because it's a single tile not part of any chow.\n\nAlternatively, if I play T6, maybe someone can Chi it if they have T5 or T7. But since I don't have those, it's less likely. But if someone has a T5 and T7, they could Chi T6. But I don't know their hands.\n\nIn conclusion, playing B8 or T6 seems safe. Between them, maybe B8 is better. So the answer would be Play B8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential to form a concealed pung with W7 and a chow with T1-T3. Playing B8 is safe as it is a single tile not part of any melds in the hand.]\nReason: [Keeping T1, T2, T3 for a chow and W7 for a possible pung is beneficial. B8 is a single tile with no immediate use in the current hand, making it a safe discard.]\nAnswer: Play B8"}
