s another chow if I have W6. But I don't. Alternatively, W7-8-9, but W8 is gone. So that's not possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe focus on the T9 and W9 as terminals. Let's see: if I can form a hand with All Terminals and Honors. That's worth 32 points. Let's check: terminals are T1, T9, W9. Honors are F4 (north wind, my seat wind), J2 (green dragon). But I have B7, which is a middle tile. So that's not possible. Need to get rid of B7. If I discard B7, then my hand has T1, T2, T3, T4, T9, W4, W5, W7, W9, F4, J2. Still, T4 is a middle tile. So not all terminals. Hmm.\n\nAnother angle: since my seat wind is North (F4), and the prevalent wind is West (F1), I have a F4 in my hand. Maybe forming a pung of F4 would give me a Seat Wind pung, which is worth 2 points. But I only have one F4. So that's not possible. Alternatively, if I can get another F4, but there's only 2 left in unshown tiles. But I can't get it unless someone discards.\n\nLooking at the valid actions: the possible plays are B7, T3, T4, T9, W4, W5, J2, T2, W7, F4, W9, T1. I need to choose which tile to discard. The key is to not give others the chance to complete their hands. For example, if I discard T4, maybe someone can chi it. Let's see: the previous player has a chow of T3-4-5. If I discard T4, they might not need it. The next player has a chow of T3-4-5 (from their melds: [T3, T4, T5]). So if I discard T4, they already have that chow. So maybe not a problem. The player across has a kong of W8, so they might not need T4.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard B7, which is a single tile, it might not help anyone. But B7 is part of a possible chow. Let's see: B7 is in my hand. The unshown B7 has 2 left. If I discard B7, maybe someone can use it in a chow. For example, B6-7-8. The previous player has a chow of B6-7-8. So if I discard B7, they might already have that chow. So maybe not a problem.\n\nLooking at my hand, if I discard B7, then I have T1, T2, T3, T4, T9, W4, W5, W7, W9, F4, J2. Wait, that's 11 tiles? 