Maybe not helpful.\n\nWait, what if I discard T4? Then I have T1-2-3 as a chow. T9 as a terminal. W4-5-7-9. Hmm. Maybe not. Alternatively, if I can form a hand with All Terminals and Honors by discarding B7 and T4. Let's see: if I discard B7 and T4, then my hand is T1 (2), T2, T3, T9, W4, W5, W7, W9, F4, J2. Still, W4 and W5 are not terminals. So not All Terminals and Honors.\n\nHmm. Maybe the best move is to discard B7, as it's a single tile that's not part of any useful meld for others. Also, keeping the T4 might help in forming a chow if I can get T5. But T5 has 2 left. If I keep T4, maybe I can chi a T5 if someone discards it. But the previous player already has a chow of T3-4-5, so they might not need it. The next player also has a T3-4-5 chow. So if I discard T4, they might already have it. So it's safer to discard B7.\n\nAnother angle: looking at the valid actions, the user can play B7, T3, T4, etc. If I discard B7, then I have T1-2-3 chow, T9, W4-5-7-9, F4, J2. Maybe this allows for a hand with All Chows. Let's see: T1-2-3 (chow), then maybe W4-5-6 (need W6), but no. Alternatively, W7-8-9 (need W8, which is gone). Not helpful. So maybe not.\n\nWait, I have W4, W5, W7, W9. If I can form a chow of W4-5-6 (if I have W6) and W7-8-9 (if I have W8). But both are missing. So maybe not. Alternatively, maybe form a hand with All Terminals and Honors by discarding T4 and B7. Then I have T1 (2), T2, T3, T9, W4, W5, W7, W9, F4, J2. Still, W4 and W5 are not terminals. So not All Terminals.\n\nHmm. Maybe the best option is to discard T4, as it's part of a possible chow, but since others already have that chow, it's safe. Or discard B7. Let's check the unshown tiles. B7 has 2 left. If I discard B7, there's a chance someone might need it. For example, B6-7-8. The previous player has a chow of B6-7-8. If they already have that chow, they don't need another B7. So it's safe to discard B7.\n\nIn terms of scoring, if I discard B7, my hand is T1-2-3 chow, T9, W4-5-7-9, F4, J2. Maybe this allows for a hand with All Chows. Let's see: T1-2-3 (chow), then maybe W4-5-6 (need W6), W7-8-9 (need W8). Not possible. Alternatively, if I can form a pair of T1, and three chows. For example, T1-2-3, W4-5-6, W7-8-9. But missing tiles. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the hand can be a mix of chows and pungs. But I don't have any pungs. So perhaps the best is to keep the T1-2-3 chow and aim for a hand with All Chows. But I need two more chows. Let's see: if I can get W6 and W8, but that's unlikely. So maybe not.\n\nAnother thought: the hand has F4 (north wind), which is my seat wind. If I can form a pung of F4, that would give me 2 points. But I only have one. So no. Alternatively, if I can get another F4, but there are two left. Maybe not.\n\nConsidering all this, the best move is to discard B7, as it's a single tile that's not part of any useful meld for others, and it allows me to keep the T1-2-3 chow. Also, keeping T4 might help if I can chi a T5, but since others already have that chow, it's less likely. So the answer is to play B7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding B7 is optimal as it avoids aiding others' melds and maintains flexibility for forming chows like T1-2-3 while preserving terminal tiles (T1, T9, W9) and honor tiles (F4, J2) for potential high-scoring combinations like All Terminals and Honors.  \nReason: B7 is a non-terminal, non-honor tile not part of any active chows in others' melds. Discarding it simplifies the hand, prioritizing terminal/honor tiles and preserving the T1-2-3 chow for possible 32-point All Terminals and Honors or 8-point Outside Hand patterns.  \nAnswer: Play B7"}
