r has a W3-W4-W5 meld. The next player has W1-W2-W3. The player across has W8 kong. So W7 is not part of any existing melds. So discarding W7 is safe.\n\nSimilarly, W9: Unshown W9 has 2 left. The next player has a W9 in their melds (they played W9, and their melds include [J3, J3, J3], [B1, B1, B1], [T1, T2, T3], but they also played W9 in their history. So they might have a W9 in their hand. But since there are two left, it's possible to discard W9.\n\nSo between W7 and W9, which is better? If I discard W7, it's a single tile. If I discard W9, it's a terminal. Since I have T9 as a terminal, maybe losing a terminal is less impactful. Or maybe keeping terminals for patterns like All Terminals or others. But since I don't have many terminals, maybe it's better to keep them.\n\nAlternatively, looking at my hand, I have W7 and W9 as single tiles. If I discard one of them, I can focus on other parts of the hand. Maybe W7 is better to discard because it's not part of any potential chow in my hand. W9 is a terminal, which could be part of a pair or a chow if I have W8 and W9, but I don't have W8.\n\nSo perhaps discarding W7 is the best option here. It's a single tile not involved in any potential melds in my hand, and it's not part of others' existing melds. That way, I don't give others a chance to form a meld, and I can keep working on my own hand.\n\nAnother consideration: if I discard W7, maybe someone can form a chow with W6, W7, W8. But W8 is already out (unshown W8:0), so no W8 left. So only W6 and W7. Since I don't have W6 in my hand (unshown W6 has 4 left), but others might have it. Not sure. But since W8 is gone, a chow of W6-W7-W8 isn't possible. So W7 is safe.\n\nTherefore, the best action is to discard W7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding W7 minimizes the risk of aiding opponents' melds and maintains potential for concealed hand points. W7 is a single tile not part of any existing or potential melds in my hand, and its discard doesn't interfere with opponents' current melds.\n\nReason: W7 is a non-critical tile that doesn't contribute to my hand's patterns. Opponents have no active melds requiring W7, and discarding it avoids giving them a chance to form new melds. This preserves my concealed hand potential and avoids exposing tiles that could be used in others' strategies.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
